<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Leucas</h1><p class="st-meta">640 BCE – 167 BCE (approx.) · capital Leucas</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Corinthian colony on the Ionian island of Lefkada, which cut a canal through its isthmus and later became the capital of the Acarnanian League.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Corinthians led by a son of the tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cypselus">Cypselus</a> founded Leucas c. 640 BCE on what was then a peninsula of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acarnania">Acarnania</a>. The colonists dug a canal through the isthmus, making it an island. Its white cliffs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-lefkatas">Cape Leucatas</a> were linked with the legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sappho">Sappho's</a> leap.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Leucas stayed close to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corinth">Corinth</a>, fighting at Salamis in 480 BCE and with Corinth at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sybota">Sybota</a> in 433 BCE, and was a member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/peloponnesian-league">Peloponnesian League</a>. It was an oligarchy living from trade along the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> it joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/acarnanian-league">Acarnanian League</a> and became its meeting place.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-macedonian-war">Third Macedonian War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> separated Leucas from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acarnania">Acarnania</a> in 167 BCE, and it came under Roman control. The island is today's Lefkada.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 640 BCE – 167 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:46.97%;width:9.41%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>640 BCE – 167 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Leucas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Apollo Leucatas</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Leukas, Lefkada</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-404&amp;focus=leucas" aria-label="Leucas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5213 776 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Leucas lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5529 1046h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5533 1032h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5533 1032h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Greece. <a href="/atlas?year=-404&amp;focus=leucas">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

